#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079675 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the graph of $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2cb61ee785ff6e04c5b73281389d6653f42bc93636da6840fbc2391ffb82451c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$, how can we use graph transformations to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2x-4\\right)+1 \\,\n$$\n\nIn this particular situation, we have to combine several operations.\n\nTo perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must apply the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nIn this case, we begin by factoring out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x-4\\right) +1 = f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNow, to plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) +1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x)$.\n\n![](images/07/07fe84bddbdc721dfba2fc9b720ca79ff794e9846fa1ef3bbc0c3c2217edd4e8.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the right by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/a1/a16ccd8e56c64f80f93476f2b7a41aa3093a2e4b9fdbe089fa502d1f1df15923.png)\n\n- There is no vertical scaling, so we omit this step.\n- Finally, translate it up by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)+1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4a894860fadbc3ac88fa360cae23ef0f33903f3d930cb00496bb2c2505d2334.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf539881-a530-50df-b39d-b9718839371f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4616 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
